{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01Y9M15Y8tp3LQyjZR7UyTLW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012keAzPV8TjUJEocJGzVSAM","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/coordinator-run/sos_226/review.json"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1000,"cache_read_input_tokens":8533,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1000,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fgerequ9qxd5J2TVXmtMNu","session_id":"eab38cf5-7da5-43e8-ba85-a59c2942584c","uuid":"af2884b1-2e06-426a-b589-4389df82758b","request_id":"req_011CbUfwDQtrRQPP4pDtyaCG","subagent_type":"Implementer","task_description":"Implementer revision for sos_226"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012keAzPV8TjUJEocJGzVSAM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n4\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n5\t    \"line\": 151,\n6\t    \"comment\": \"_apply_casualty_for_spell is never hooked into the casting pipeline. register_triggers is a no-op and there is no on_cast override. The casualty mechanic therefore never fires during actual gameplay — only the tests call _apply_casualty_for_spell directly. Per the spec ('each instant and sorcery spell you cast has casualty 1'), the card must override on_cast to detect whether a matching spell is being cast and invoke _apply_casualty_for_spell, or register a trigger/hook via register_triggers. Without this integration the feature is dead code in a real game.\"\n7\t  },\n8\t  {\n9\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n10\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n11\t    \"line\": 98,\n12\t    \"comment\": \"_apply_casualty_for_spell resolves the spell's controller with 'getattr(spell, \\\"controller\\\", None) or getattr(self, \\\"controller\\\", None)'. This means if the spell legitimately has controller=None (e.g. before the casting pipeline sets it), the method falls back silently to Silverquill's controller without any warning. More critically, this fallback makes the method apply casualty to ANY instant/sorcery that happens to lack a controller attribute while Silverquill is on the battlefield, even opponent spells. The correct fix is to use only spell.controller, falling back to self.controller only if truly necessary and documenting why.\"\n13\t  },\n14\t  {\n15\t    \"severity\": \"strict\",\n16\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n17\t    \"line\": 142,\n18\t    \"comment\": \"copy.copy(spell) creates a shallow copy. For a StackObject-backed spell, the copied_card shares mutable state (e.g. chosen_targets list, card_types set) with the original. If on_resolve mutates these (e.g. appending to chosen_targets), it corrupts the original spell's state mid-resolution. engine/stack.py already provides copy_spell() which does the same shallow copy but is the established engine utility. Using copy_spell(game, original_stack_obj, ctrl) would be both correct and consistent with the codebase convention.\"\n19\t  },\n20\t  {\n21\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n22\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n23\t    \"line\": 109,\n24\t    \"comment\": \"Silverquill itself is excluded from being a valid casualty sacrifice target (c is not self). The actual Magic rules for casualty 1 allow sacrificing any creature with power >= 1 that you control, including the permanent granting casualty. While this does not affect the tests, it is a rules deviation that would surface in actual gameplay.\"\n25\t  },\n26\t  {\n27\t    \"severity\": \"advisory\",\n28\t    \"file\": \"cards/sos/sos_226/card_impl.py\",\n29\t    \"line\": 124,\n30\t    \"comment\": \"The bare 'except (ScriptExhaustedError, Exception)' clause silently swallows all exceptions (since Exception is a supertype of ScriptExhaustedError, the explicit listing of ScriptExhaustedError is redundant). Swallowing all exceptions defaults wants_casualty=False, which can hide real bugs (e.g. AttributeError on ctrl). Consider only catching ScriptExhaustedError here.\"\n31\t  }\n32\t]\n33\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fgerequ9qxd5J2TVXmtMNu","session_id":"eab38cf5-7da5-43e8-ba85-a59c2942584c","uuid":"8ff1e852-d83b-486d-85d9-fad65a8fa6a1","timestamp":"2026-05-28T08:44:15.918Z","subagent_type":"Implementer","task_description":"Implementer revision for sos_226"}
